{
  "reasoning": "The lecture moves from positioning empirical evaluation in system implementation to explaining what an empirical usability study requires, its four stages, and then uses a text-entry example to cover research-question framing and variable identification. I cut before sentence 24 where the lecturer shifts from defining empirical study to comparing it with earlier quick prototype evaluations, before 45 where the four stages are introduced, before 67 where the example begins, before 186 where the second stage, variable identification, begins, and before 211 where the closing summary begins. The research-question example is kept as one segment because the later discussion of RQ3 and testable questions depends on the earlier RQ1 and RQ2 examples and together they form the research-question stage.",
  "segments": [
